  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday Night through Thursday)\nIssued at 1143 PM CDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\nRain chances will increase and become more widespread Friday \nnight and through much of Saturday as the mid-level shortwave \ntracks directly over the forecast area. Instability remains very \nlimited, with CAPE values generally under 300 J/kg through the \ndaytime Saturday. This should keep thunder probs low, but non- \nzero...generally 10 percent or lower. Light to moderate showers \nwill gradually decrease from west-to-east Saturday \nafternoon/evening before ending early Sunday morning. Rainfall \namounts Friday through Sunday morning should be less than half an \ninch, with only the NAM showing pockets of higher amounts in \nplaces. \n\nA brief lull in precipitation is expected Sunday into early \nMonday as the shortwave clears and weak mid-level ridging builds \nin. This will allow afternoon highs to rebound into the mid-to-\nupper 70s under partly sunny/mostly cloudy skies.\n\nAttention then shifts to an increasingly dynamic system slated \nfor early next week. Global deterministic models continue to \nsignal an amplified upper-level trough moving onshore across the \nWest Coast. This trough will merge with a secondary upper-level \nlow over the Desert Southwest Sunday night into Monday morning. \nThe resultant trough and its axis will move over the Southern \nPlains by Monday afternoon, increasing instability and drastically\nspreading deep moisture into the area. Shower and thunderstorm \nchances will trend higher and spread from northwest to southeast \nbeginning early Monday morning. Model consensus indicates the \nhighest chances and heaviest rain will occur along and just ahead \nof an approaching surface cold front Monday evening/night and into\nTuesday morning. Instability, bulk shear, and a plethora of \nmoisture will all be sufficient for a limited potential for strong\nand/or severe thunderstorms; exact details will be refined over \nthe next few days. Anomalously high PWATs will surge into the area\nlate Sunday night, increasing to over 1.5 inches and peaking near\n2.0 inches along the cold front. This will likely result in heavy\nrain, especially Monday evening/night and into Tuesday morning \nwhen the heaviest rain is expected to fall. Isolated flash \nflooding will be possible. There is still considerable \nmodel/ensemble variance regarding the exact timing of the front, \nwith the NBM solution showing it pushing into the CWA sometime \nmid-morning Tuesday. In the wake of the front, high pressure will\nbring a noticeable, seasonable fall cooldown through the \nremainder of the forecast period, with highs dropping back into \nthe mid-to-upper 60s and lower 70s and widespread overnight lows \nfalling into the 40s.\n\nMejia",
